text:
maria: Quick design question — thinking about adding a horizontal swipe/scrub (trackpad + mouse horizontal scroll) to the version history timeline so power users can skim past commits fast. Could be handy, but worried about accidental rollbacks when people click-drag. Thoughts? https://figma.com/file/xxxx (mock)
jon: I like the idea for speed. Two immediate concerns: (1) wheel/gesture events differ a lot between macOS/Windows/Chromebook, (2) our variant chips have small hit targets — we'd need to enlarge them otherwise mouse drags will trigger clicks. Would prototype with deltaX handling and pointer-over check.
leah: Accessibility flag: gestures are non-discoverable and must never replace keyboard controls. Keep Left/Right + Home/End navigation. Make sure hit targets are >=44px and there is a clear focus state. Also: previews only — do not perform destructive actions (rollback) on swipe. Use an aria-live region to announce the previewed version.
sam: +1. Product asks: (a) gesture should be preview-only, (b) committing requires an explicit interaction (Apply / Confirm modal), (c) show a visible affordance so people know the timeline is draggable. Prefer tooltip copy: "Drag to preview versions — click Apply to publish".
jon: Implementation sketch: listen for wheel/pointer with a small deadzone and only scrub when cursor is over timeline. CSS note: `touch-action: pan-y;` to avoid interfering with vertical scroll on touch devices. Quick prototype snippet:
```
// only scrub on horizontal intent
timeline.addEventListener('wheel', e => {
  if (Math.abs(e.deltaX) > 6 && Math.abs(e.deltaX) > Math.abs(e.deltaY)) {
    scrub(Math.sign(e.deltaX));
  }
});
```
maria: UI idea — show a faint "grab" handle on hover to signal drag-ability, and keep a visible keyboard focus ring for the current version. Tooltip copy draft: "Drag to scrub history — changes are previews until you click Apply". Does that read OK?
leah: Minor copy tweak: "Preview changes — click Apply to make them live." For SR: avoid verbs like "scrub"; use "browse" or "preview". Accessibility announce example (prefer aria-live):
```
<div id="timeline-live" aria-live="polite" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px"></div>
function announce(txt){ document.getElementById('timeline-live').textContent = txt; }
// call announce(`Previewing version ${versionId}`)
```
jon: I'll wire that up. Also will ensure timeline only sends announce events when the previewed item changes, debounce 200ms.
sam: UX detail: protect against one-off accidental commits — keep primary CTA as Apply, and require modifier (e.g., hold Shift) + click for immediate rollback in advanced mode. And color/contrast: rollback/rollback-confirm should be a high-contrast red in modal only.
aria-bot: Accessibility checklist run: keyboard nav present, aria-live region detected, target size check flagged (some chips <44px) — action: enlarge chips, ensure focus styles. All other a11y items ✅
maria: Great — @jon can you drop a quick interactive demo (gif or small figma prototype) in this thread? I'll update copy and add the grab handle visual. Thanks everyone :sparkles:
